      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Daniel J. Eisenstein",
        "Shaun Cole"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "BAO turned a feature of early-universe physics into a precise standard ruler, enabling powerful tests of cosmic expansion and dark energy. It is a major, enduring observational advance.",
        "attribution": "Eisenstein and Cole led landmark independent observational detections and measurements. Sunyaev's foundational theoretical contributions deserve recognition, though the proposed discovery centers on establishing BAO as a practical cosmological ruler.",
        "maturity": "Repeated surveys have measured the feature across cosmic epochs with increasing precision; the result is well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The boundary between the theoretical prediction and the later observational achievement complicates a three-person attribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alan H. Guth",
        "Andrei Linde",
        "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Inflation offers a remarkably fruitful account of early-universe conditions, while quantum fluctuations provide a physical origin for the structures later seen in the cosmos. This is foundational cosmology.",
        "attribution": "Guth and Linde were central to developing inflation; Mukhanov made seminal contributions to the quantum theory of cosmological perturbations. The credited trio captures distinct essential parts of the achievement.",
        "maturity": "The structure-generation framework is strongly supported by observations, but inflation as a specific early-universe epoch has not been uniquely established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "No unique inflationary model or decisive primordial gravitational-wave signature has been confirmed."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Federico Capasso",
        "Jérôme Faist",
        "Alfred Y. Cho"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser made intersubband transitions a practical route to compact, tunable sources, opening important spectral ranges and enabling extensive applications.",
        "attribution": "Capasso and Faist were central to the device concept and its realization; Cho's semiconductor heterostructure expertise was crucial to making the design possible.",
        "maturity": "The technology has been developed, reproduced, and used broadly over decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The precise division of conceptual and engineering credit among the three merits careful historical review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Ursula Keller",
        "Wilson Sibbett",
        "Peter F. Moulton"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Titanium-sapphire lasers and passive mode-locking enabled reliable ultrashort pulses and transformed ultrafast science, from nonlinear optics to precision measurement.",
        "attribution": "Moulton developed the key laser medium, while Sibbett and Keller made major contributions to passive mode-locking and its practical performance. The combined discovery has several connected strands.",
        "maturity": "The techniques and laser platform have been central to research and applications for many years.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "A single prize citation would need to distinguish the separate contributions to the gain medium and mode-locking."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Maldacena",
        "Edward Witten"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Gauge/gravity duality is a profound conceptual bridge between quantum field theory and gravity that has reshaped work across high-energy theory and condensed matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Maldacena formulated the influential duality; Witten clarified and greatly extended its structure. Polyakov's earlier work helped establish important ingredients, so the full history deserves close attention.",
        "maturity": "The correspondence has been extensively developed and tested within mathematical and theoretical frameworks, but no direct empirical test of quantum gravity follows from it.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its status as a physical description of our universe, rather than a powerful theoretical correspondence, remains unsettled.",
          "The earliest conceptual contributions and the later formulation are difficult to separate."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Maura McLaughlin",
        "Michael Kramer",
        "Richard N. Manchester"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Pulsar timing arrays have opened the nanohertz gravitational-wave band and found compelling evidence for a background, a major extension of gravitational-wave astronomy.",
        "attribution": "The proposed names represent essential leadership in long-running pulsar timing efforts. The achievement is collective across international collaborations, requiring care in identifying individual contributions.",
        "maturity": "Multiple arrays report consistent evidence, but the signal's detailed properties and astrophysical interpretation are still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The background's source population and any contribution from sources beyond supermassive black-hole binaries remain uncertain."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Enrico Costa",
        "Filippo Frontera",
        "Shrinivas R. Kulkarni"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Identifying gamma-ray burst afterglows and their cosmological origin transformed brief high-energy transients into probes of the distant universe.",
        "attribution": "Costa and Frontera were central to the early afterglow breakthrough; Kulkarni led key optical follow-up and helped establish the cosmological interpretation. The sequence of discoveries involved many teams.",
        "maturity": "The afterglow picture and cosmological distances are well established, though the field continues to uncover varied progenitors and emission mechanisms.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Credit for the rapid, multiwavelength breakthrough is distributed across several observatories and teams."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B017",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The theoretical foundations for trapped-ion quantum computing and atomic quantum simulation gave concrete form to quantum information processing and controllable many-body physics.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller made closely linked, foundational contributions to quantum gates and simulation with trapped ions.",
        "maturity": "These ideas have guided decades of experimental progress, though scalable fault-tolerant quantum computation remains a developing goal.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The enduring scientific importance is clear, but the balance between foundational proposals and later experimental achievements needs consideration."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
